{
  "id": "Compound::DB00304 - Gene::5241",
  "verbalization": "Desogestrel, a progestin analog, is linked to Pgr through its inclusion in the Progesterone Congeners class, which includes compounds like Norelgestromin, Norgestimate, and Ethynodiol. These compounds all bind to Pgr, suggesting a common mechanism of action involving the progesterone receptor. The lowest common ancestors of Desogestrel and its congeners are both Progestin and Steroid, reinforcing the shared biological context of these molecules. The evidence indicates that Desogestrel, through its structural similarity to progesterone analogs, may exert its effects by binding to Pgr, thereby modulating its downstream signaling. This relationship is supported by the consistent presence of Pgr as a target across multiple pathways, highlighting a potential role in progesterone receptor-mediated processes. The integration of these pathways underscores the importance of progestin analogs in influencing Pgr activity, potentially through shared receptor binding or downstream signaling cascades.",
